      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">How is SGPA (Semester GPA) Calculated?</h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
        Semester Grade Point Average (SGPA) is the official metric used by colleges and universities to measure your academic performance over a single term (semester or quarter). Unlike a simple grade average, SGPA is a <strong>credit-weighted average</strong>. This means that a course with higher credit hours has a larger mathematical impact on your final average.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-2">The SGPA Formula</h3>
      <div class="p-6 bg-indigo-50/50 dark:bg-indigo-950/20 rounded-xl border border-indigo-100 dark:border-indigo-900/30 text-center font-mono text-base sm:text-lg text-indigo-700 dark:text-indigo-400">
        SGPA = Sum of (Grade Points × Course Credits) ÷ Total Credits
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-2">Step-by-Step Calculation Example</h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        Suppose you take 4 courses in a semester: Math (4 credits, Grade Point 9.0), Physics Lab (1.5 credits, Grade Point 10.0), CS (3 credits, Grade Point 8.0), and Technical English (2 credits, Grade Point 7.0).
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 ml-4">
        <li><strong>Math:</strong> 4.0 × 9.0 = 36.0 weighted points</li>
        <li><strong>Physics Lab:</strong> 1.5 × 10.0 = 15.0 weighted points</li>
        <li><strong>Computer Science:</strong> 3.0 × 8.0 = 24.0 weighted points</li>
        <li><strong>Technical English:</strong> 2.0 × 7.0 = 14.0 weighted points</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">
        Summing these up, total quality points = 36 + 15 + 24 + 14 = <strong>89.0</strong>. Total credit hours = 4.0 + 1.5 + 3.0 + 2.0 = <strong>10.5</strong>.
        By dividing total points by total credits: 89.0 ÷ 10.5 = <strong>8.48 SGPA</strong>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- CGPA Guide -->
    <article id="guide-panel-cgpa" class="guide-panel hidden max-w-3xl space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">How to Calculate CGPA: A Step-by-Step Academic Guide</h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
        Your Cumulative Grade Point Average (CGPA) serves as a standardized measurement of your overall academic performance across semesters. Whether you are using a <strong>CGPA calculator</strong> to estimate your grades or looking to compute it manually, understanding the underlying <strong>CGPA calculation formula</strong> is essential.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-2">The Standard CGPA Formula</h3>
      <div class="p-6 bg-indigo-50/50 dark:bg-indigo-950/20 rounded-xl border border-indigo-100 dark:border-indigo-900/30 text-center font-mono text-base sm:text-lg text-indigo-700 dark:text-indigo-400">
        CGPA = Total (Semester GPA × Semester Credits) ÷ Total Credits
      </div>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        Let us look at a worked example of how a student can perform a <strong>semester GPA to CGPA</strong> calculation. Suppose you have completed three semesters:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 ml-4">
        <li><strong>Semester 1:</strong> GPA of 8.20 with 20 credits (Product = 164.0)</li>
        <li><strong>Semester 2:</strong> GPA of 7.80 with 22 credits (Product = 171.6)</li>
        <li><strong>Semester 3:</strong> GPA of 8.50 with 18 credits (Product = 153.0)</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed mt-4">
        Total quality points = 164 + 171.6 + 153 = 488.6 quality points. Total credits attempted = 20 + 22 + 18 = 60. By applying the formula: 488.6 ÷ 60 = <strong>8.14 CGPA</strong>.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- Percentage Guide -->
    <article id="guide-panel-percentage" class="guide-panel hidden max-w-3xl space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-white">How to Convert CGPA to Percentage: Standard & Official Formulas</h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed text-sm sm:text-base">
        Converting your Cumulative Grade Point Average (CGPA) to an aggregate percentage is a common task when applying for government jobs, postgraduate courses, and standard employment opportunities. Because grading metrics vary globally, there is no single conversion factor. Instead, each university board or education department mandates their own official formula.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-2">1. The CBSE & UGC India Formula (10-Point Scale)</h3>
      <div class="p-5 my-4 bg-indigo-50/50 dark:bg-indigo-950/20 rounded-xl border border-indigo-100 dark:border-indigo-900/30 text-center font-mono text-base sm:text-lg text-indigo-700 dark:text-indigo-400">
        Percentage (%) = CGPA × 9.5
      </div>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If a student scores a CGPA of <strong>8.80</strong> under the CBSE system, their percentage equivalent is 8.80 × 9.5 = <strong>83.6%</strong>.
      </p>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-2">2. The Standard US & International Formula (4.0 Scale)</h3>
      <div class="p-5 my-4 bg-indigo-50/50 dark:bg-indigo-950/20 rounded-xl border border-indigo-100 dark:border-indigo-900/30 text-center font-mono text-base sm:text-lg text-indigo-700 dark:text-indigo-400">
        Percentage (%) = GPA × 25
      </div>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Example:</strong> A student graduating with a GPA of <strong>3.60</strong> has a percentage equivalent of 3.60 × 25 = <strong>90.0%</strong>.
      </p>
    </article>
